### 1. Task outcome (short version):
Screened 100 studies (indices 101-200). Results: 6 included, 94 excluded. The 6 included studies are: Study 103 (Finnish
Mental Hospital study - 12-year CHD trial), and Studies 169, 171, 172, 173, 174 (all Lyon Diet Heart Study papers 
reporting mortality/CVD outcomes with Mediterranean diet intervention).

### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):
**Selection Criteria Applied:**
1. Randomised trial
2. Intention to reduce saturated fat intake OR intention to alter dietary fats achieving reduction in saturated fat
3. Compared with higher saturated fat intake or usual diet
4. Not multifactorial
5. Adult humans with/without CVD (not acutely ill, pregnant, breastfeeding)
6. Intervention duration ≥24 months
7. Mortality or cardiovascular morbidity data available

**INCLUDED STUDIES (6):**

| Study | Title | Key Features |
|-------|-------|--------------|
| 103 | Finnish Mental Hospital study | 12-year RCT (6+6 crossover), SCL diet vs normal diet, CHD incidence + coronary 
deaths measured |
| 169 | Mediterranean alpha-linolenic acid-rich diet | Lyon Diet Heart Study, mean 27-month follow-up, cardiac deaths 
(16vs3) + non-fatal MIs (17vs5), RR=0.27 |
| 171 | Final report Lyon Diet Heart Study | Mean 46-month follow-up, composite outcomes cardiac death+MI (14vs44), 
adjusted RR 0.28-0.53 |
| 172 | Effect of Mediterranean diet on CV complications | Mean 27-month follow-up, major endpoints CV death+nonfatal 
MI, 76% risk reduction (59vs14 events) |
| 173 | Mediterranean dietary pattern randomised trial | 4-year follow-up, total deaths (24vs14), cardiac deaths 
(19vs6), adjusted RR 0.56 for total deaths |
| 174 | Cretan Mediterranean diet prevention | Mean 27-month follow-up, recurrent MI/CV events/cardiac death decreased 
>70% |

**EXCLUDED STUDIES (94):**
Primary exclusion reasons:
- Duration <24 months: ~60 studies (most common reason)
- No mortality/CVD morbidity outcomes: ~25 studies (only intermediate risk factors measured)
- Not randomised: ~8 studies (crossover designs, feeding studies, observational)
- Multifactorial interventions: ~1 study (Finnish Diabetes Prevention Study)
- Drug trials not dietary interventions: ~2 studies (LRC-CPPT cholestyramine trials)
- Wrong outcomes: ~8 studies (breast cancer, MS, polyps, etc.)

### 3. Additional context:
The Lyon Diet Heart Study appears multiple times (studies 169-174) as different publications from the same trial 
reporting various analyses. These were all included as they each present unique outcome data meeting the selection 
criteria. Study 170 was excluded as it is a methodology paper describing bias control techniques without presenting 
primary outcomes.

The most stringent criterion was the 24-month minimum duration combined with requirement for actual mortality or CVD 
morbidity outcomes (not just risk factor changes). This eliminated the vast majority of short-term metabolic ward 
studies and feeding trials that only measured lipid profiles or other intermediate markers.


